  <div class="art-tag">Essays from the Studio</div>
  <h1 class="art-title">On Pressing Everything Against the Canvas</h1>
  <div class="art-meta">June 2026 · Berlin</div>

  <div class="art-body">
    <p>OBOSHI describes the repetitive, unhurried act of tearing and repositioning washi tape by hand as less like hesitation and more like a standing question, and notes a recent shift away from working toward compositional harmony and toward wanting to press everything he is directly against the canvas instead.</p>
    <p>What he's after now, he writes, isn't agreement between his own vibration and the painting's but an honest friction between the two. A dialogue with Claude reframes this not as recklessness but as a form of self-trust, contrasting the outward-looking search for harmony — toward a viewer, toward some standard of beauty — with the inward focus of pressing everything in.</p>
    <p>He describes washi tape and layered sheets of clear plastic as holding a kind of memory distinct from acrylic paint — present without asserting themselves, not unlike things inside him that haven't yet become language.</p>
    <p>The essay closes by reframing the entire layered process — applying, pressing, scraping back, layering again — not as a route toward a finished, harmonious composition but as a way of uncovering what's already there. Harmony might emerge along the way, he writes, but it was never the actual destination. Dated Berlin, June 2026.</p>
  </div>

